WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.576
Key management is often the hardest part of practical cryptography.

2
00:00:04.576 --> 00:00:08.265
Choosing a maintained algorithm is only the beginning.

3
00:00:08.265 --> 00:00:22.473
The system also needs to generate keys safely, store them safely, grant access narrowly, rotate them deliberately, back them up where appropriate, recover from failures, and retire them when no longer needed.

4
00:00:22.473 --> 00:00:32.787
Keys need lifecycle management: generation, storage, use, access control, rotation, revocation, backup, recovery, destruction, and compromise response.

5
00:00:32.787 --> 00:00:34.631
Each stage needs ownership.

6
00:00:34.631 --> 00:00:41.598
A key with no owner or rotation path becomes an operational liability even if the algorithm is strong.

7
00:00:41.598 --> 00:00:45.833
Development, staging, and production keys should be separated.

8
00:00:45.833 --> 00:00:56.830
Environment separation reduces blast radius, prevents test behavior from affecting production, and allows rotation or containment without unnecessary disruption.

9
00:00:56.830 --> 00:01:01.475
Shared keys across unrelated environments create avoidable coupling.

10
00:01:01.475 --> 00:01:11.789
Keys and secrets should not be stored in source control, logs, public configuration, build output, screenshots, support tickets, or client-side assets.

11
00:01:11.789 --> 00:01:17.869
These locations are copied and retained through normal engineering and support workflows.

12
00:01:17.869 --> 00:01:21.899
Secret storage should be intentional and access-controlled.

13
00:01:21.899 --> 00:01:27.978
Rotation should be planned so dependent systems can continue operating during transition.

14
00:01:27.978 --> 00:01:31.461
Some systems need overlap between old and new keys.

15
00:01:31.461 --> 00:01:37.336
Others need versioned keys, key identifiers, dual-read behavior, or phased deployment.

16
00:01:37.336 --> 00:01:41.639
The rotation plan should be designed before rotation is urgent.

17
00:01:41.639 --> 00:01:50.655
Cloud key stores or HSM-backed services may reduce direct key exposure, but they still require permissions and lifecycle governance.

18
00:01:50.655 --> 00:01:58.579
Access policies, managed identity, audit logs, network configuration, backup, and emergency procedures still matter.

19
00:01:58.579 --> 00:02:06.912
Key compromise response should include containment, rotation, revocation, monitoring, communication, and follow-up review.

20
00:02:06.912 --> 00:02:14.972
The response plan should identify which data, tokens, services, and dependent systems may be affected by a key change.
